---
import '@fontsource/manrope/400.css';
import '@fontsource/manrope/500.css';
import '@fontsource/manrope/600.css';
import '@fontsource/manrope/700.css';
import '../../styles/global.css';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Car Buying Fees to Review | Smart Auto Buying</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Understand which car buying fees deserve a second look before you sign a purchase agreement."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <main class="guide-page">
      <a class="guide-back" href="/#faq">Back to guides</a>

      <article class="guide-article">
        <header class="guide-hero">
          <p class="guide-kicker">Fee check</p>
          <h1>What fees deserve a second look before you sign</h1>
          <figure class="guide-image">
            <img
              src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1560472355-536de3962603?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&q=85"
              alt="A person reviewing documents before signing"
            />
          </figure>
          <p class="guide-lede">
            Some fees are normal. Some are negotiable. Some are add-ons dressed up to look mandatory.
            Knowing the difference can protect hundreds or thousands of dollars.
          </p>
        </header>

        <section>
          <h2>Documentation fees</h2>
          <p>
            A documentation fee covers paperwork processing. In many states it is common, and in
            some places it may be capped or consistently charged by a dealer. It is still worth
            looking at because a high doc fee can offset an advertised discount.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Dealer add-ons</h2>
          <p>
            Nitrogen, paint protection, fabric protection, VIN etching, wheel locks, theft recovery,
            and similar products may appear on a quote as if they are required. Ask whether each
            item can be removed, declined, or discounted.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Government fees</h2>
          <p>
            Taxes, title, registration, and plate fees are generally not dealer profit. Still, they
            should be clear and consistent with your state and county. If a line item is vague, ask
            for the exact purpose of the charge.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Finance office products</h2>
          <p>
            Extended warranties, GAP, prepaid maintenance, tire and wheel coverage, and service
            contracts can be useful in the right situation, but they should be chosen deliberately.
            Do not evaluate them under time pressure at signing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <aside class="guide-callout">
          <strong>Smart Auto Buying tip</strong>
          <p>
            Before you sign, ask for a final itemized buyer's order. Every fee should have a plain
            explanation.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </article>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
